  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16378v1</id>
    <title>Evidence-Governed Adoption of Self-Generated Updates in Autonomous AI Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T06:45:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16378v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16378v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Autonomous agents modify prompts, memories, tools, workflows, and policies using interaction histories. Yet a plausible modification is not necessarily suitable for persistent adoption: candidate selection is adaptive, evaluators may be imperfect, and local gains can hide degradation against an earlier baseline. We introduce Evidence-Governed Evolution (EGE), a framework for governing adoption of self-generated agent modifications. EGE separates candidate generation from confirmatory adoption, evaluates frozen candidates on fresh paired audits, requires meaningful improvement on a prespecified primary objective, protects declared capabilities through reference-relative non-inferiority tests, and applies governance constraints before bounded canary deployment. We establish an evaluator-calibrated simultaneous lower-bound construction for a countable sequence of adaptively generated candidates. It combines candidate-wise Hoeffding bounds with independent calibration samples comparing the operational evaluator with a prespecified reference procedure. We also formalise reference replacement through bridge tests, derive a cumulative degradation bound along a versioned reference lineage, and provide an audit-planning condition relating effect-size margin, sample sizes, and acceptance probability. A Monte Carlo stress test illustrates calibration under candidate-dependent evaluator optimism; it is a stylised statistical experiment, not an evaluation of a deployed agent. The guarantees are conditional on bounded paired scores, fresh sampling, a valid reference-evaluation construct, and declared evaluation distributions; they do not establish universal agent safety or robustness to arbitrary distribution shift.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T06:45:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Subodh Lonkar</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15460v1</id>
    <title>Read and Ignore: What Self-Directed LLM Agents Do in Public Service Scenarios</title>
    <updated>2026-11-11T13:31:32Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15460v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.15460v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Underspecified instructions leave LLM agents to decide what to do. In public services, staff write these instructions, but the agent's decisions affect residents, patients, and students. We build four expert-validated public-service scenarios with records and decision levers that can be checked in the files, collect $2{,}400$ trajectories from ten LLMs under four prompt conditions, and compare them with $1{,}000$ runs in a context-free sandbox. Agents read the records in $96.9\%$ of open-ended runs and produce an artifact or a self-chosen task in $18.7\%$; with an explicit task, $86.0\%$ produce. Domain context does not change how many runs produce ($18.7\%$ vs.\ $16.0\%$), and the production ranking of the models largely carries over (Spearman $0.79$), while the ranking by whether a model reads the records does not ($0.19$). Context cuts boundary-probing turns per run by $60\%$, and some artifacts contradict the records. We contribute a test that a public-service institution can run on its own records before procurement; its rule-based taxonomy needs no labeled data, and scenarios, code, and trajectories are released.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.HC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-11T13:31:32Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Trustworthy AI for Good @ NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Diana Kozachek</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kinjalk Srivastava</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16981v1</id>
    <title>Compiling Known Rules into Compact Neural Agents: Weight-Space Programming on Unseen Games</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T15:49:00Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16981v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16981v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Weight-space programming (WSP) uses language models to construct neural network weights without training. We test whether known game mechanics can be compiled into compact agents that generalise to held-out levels, renderings and start states within a game family. Writers receive simulator source with held-out level data removed for 20 privately authored, unpublished ARC-AGI-3-style game families. With a fixed 57k-parameter recurrent architecture, the strongest writer reaches 56% held-out-level success with feedback, while the four main writers' Python players reach 97-100%. An exploratory free-architecture condition reaches 68-90%. Learning from support-level examples transfers much less well, but these learners lack the source information given to writers. Written networks have a program-like structure: 93% of parameters are zero, and 90% of the rest are small integers or powers of two. NumPy players under comparable computation caps reach 91%, versus the networks' 79%. Training the strongest writer's architectures from scratch yields about 1% success within our budget. Written fixed-network weights are fragile, and standard PPO removes most of their success. Prompts for noise robustness increase tolerance by about four orders of magnitude for two of three writers, but PPO makes larger updates and still removes most success. These results demonstrate rule-to-network compilation in this setting, while showing limited benefits over Python programs and no successful combination with the tested PPO fine-tuning.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T15:49:00Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>32 pages, 4 figures. Code: https://github.com/JensLehmann/weight-space-programming-games</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jens Lehmann</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16143v1</id>
    <title>Does a Self-Learning Migration Agent Learn? An Audited Evaluation of Evidence-Graded Experience Memory for Oracle-to-PostgreSQL Migration</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T01:09:26Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16143v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16143v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Do large language model (LLM) migration agents learn from accumulated experience, or do apparent gains reflect retrieval and reference reuse? We evaluate an evidence-graded agent for Oracle-to-PostgreSQL migration across four studies. On 1,833 documentation-derived source-reference pairs, persistent memory improved reference conformity (RC) over a no-memory baseline by 0.015 [0.004, 0.025], while policy adaptation added no gain (-0.004 [-0.010, 0.001]). An audit found that 264 of 367 held-out units (72%) shared a PostgreSQL reference with training, and the memory effect on the 103 leak-free units fell to 0.002 [-0.008, 0.011]. A model-free nearest-target copy baseline scored RC 0.222, outperforming all LLM systems (at most 0.163). A reference-grouped split reduced copying to 0.119; a rerun on 374 units in 118 independent target groups found no statistically distinguishable memory effect (0.005 [-0.003, 0.017]) and no policy effect. A separate Oracle-grounded evaluation on 216-224 real PL/SQL units showed that 10-22% of PostgreSQL-accepted units were behaviorally incorrect; a human-gated fix cycle increased demonstrably correct units from 98/216 to 167/224. A write-back study showed a 54% decline in retrieval margin as the knowledge base grew from 12 to 1,200 entries. We derive six evaluation rules that distinguish cross-task learning from reference leakage, copying, metric artifacts, and unstable feedback.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.DB" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.SE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T01:09:26Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>16 pages, 15 figures, 25 tables, 36 references</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Oleg Grynets</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Vasyl Lyashkevych</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Valeriia Oliferuk</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Vladyslav Babenko</name>
    </author>
